Osterwalder. And if you're an entrepreneur the book on the

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.419
right is almost now a fundamental text - and I say text even

00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:46.379
though most people now have kindles. I think there's

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:51.219
probably a good startup to be had to make fake spines for

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:54.399
all the books you now have on your Kindle cause there's no

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.959
way to tell when you graduate from Stanford what you

00:00:57.979 --> 00:01:01.659
actually have read. So, if anybody's looking for an idea for

00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:07.469
a startup. That's a joke. So, I guess in this class I'll

00:01:07.489 --> 00:01:10.810
have to tell you when to laugh, so I will.

00:01:10.829 --> 00:01:13.989
So, I also write a blog. Anybody ever see it? It's called

00:01:13.989 --> 00:01:17.510
SteveBlank.com. How many of you are entrepreneurs or want to

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:22.260
be? So, every possible way to screw up a startup is in here

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.959
- most of them done by me. So, if you want some stories on

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:31.569
how I've actually built some companies and then in the blog

00:01:31.589 --> 00:01:35.500
I kind of expand to some thoughts about entrepreneurship

00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:38.959
since I first wrote the test, "Four Steps to the Epiphany".

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:43.179
So, I want to tell a few short stories today - the first

00:01:43.199 --> 00:01:47.429
one, an observation about the democratization of

00:01:47.449 --> 00:01:51.689
entrepreneurship. I've been in Silicon Valley now for 33

00:01:51.709 --> 00:01:56.650
years. Hard to actually remember back that far, even harder

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:02.879
to admit but in the last 33 years entrepreneurship and its

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:09.249
twin venture capital has changed dramatically but subtly. I

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:13.829
don't think we've even understood how dramatic those changes

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:17.699
are. And I thought I'd spend the first part of this talk

00:02:17.719 --> 00:02:22.419
actually going through those changes and then talking about

00:02:22.439 --> 00:02:25.099
one of the techniques and one of the pieces in

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.159
entrepreneurial education that's been one of the catalysts

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.180
of change.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.659
So, I just want to remind you what some of the constraints

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:42.599
are for startups. When entrepreneurs first came to this

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:49.529
valley - '60's, '70's, '80's and even the 1990's - it cost a

00:02:49.549 --> 00:02:52.999
lot of money to start a company. How many of you were in

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:58.339
software startups? You know, hopefully a good number of you

00:02:58.359 --> 00:03:03.459
in software started with your credit card. But in the old

00:03:03.479 --> 00:03:08.939
days if you were in the software business step one was buy

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:12.789
expensive workstations, step two was buy expensive

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:17.489
development tools. It cost millions of dollars even for a

00:03:17.509 --> 00:03:24.069
software company to get started pre turn of the century.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.609
Some of this is still true in hardware domains, but it was

00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:33.139
also just an implicit fact of every type of startup. It cost

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:39.669
millions to start a company. It took a long time to get to

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:46.429
first customership. Engineering was done using something

00:03:46.449 --> 00:03:51.729
called waterfall development. I would spec the product, make

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.249
sure it had an entirely long and perfect list of features

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:00.249
making sure I got that list by looking at all of the

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:03.149
competitors features, making sure mine was at least three or

00:04:03.169 --> 00:04:08.209
four longer, handing that list to Engineering who would go

00:04:08.229 --> 00:04:12.859
off and go implement for something called Version 1

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:18.500
sometimes months, more than likely a year or two from the

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.280
day you started.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.230
This waterfall development methodology was about all we knew

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:33.069
until the 1990's. Customers took a long time to adopt. First

00:04:33.089 --> 00:04:37.740
customers in Silicon Valley were government subcontractors.

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:44.649
They did not buy in days or weeks. It took months if not

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:47.180
years to get an order.

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:51.860
The next wave of customers in Silicon Valley were companies.

00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:55.959
Again, these were long sales cycles and the number of

00:04:55.979 --> 00:05:00.420
customers for Silicon Valley companies could have been

00:05:00.439 --> 00:05:04.610
measured in hundreds, maybe thousands.

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:05.810
High Startup Failure Rate

00:05:05.829 --> 00:05:13.560
For 40 years the implicit notion was that startups were

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:19.009
smaller versions of large companies and that all you did in

00:05:19.029 --> 00:05:23.569
a startup in a board meeting - reporting to your Board - was

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:29.170
focus on your execution of the business plan that your VC's

00:05:29.189 --> 00:05:31.649
had bought into. "Here's what we're supposed to do. Here's

00:05:31.649 --> 00:05:33.839
when we're supposed to ship. Here's the orders we're

00:05:33.839 --> 00:05:36.579
supposed to get. Here's the revenue we're supposed to have."

00:05:36.579 --> 00:05:39.659
And by the way, there were a limited number of these VC's.

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:44.740
3000 Santo Road there's maybe 20 of them up until about

00:05:44.759 --> 00:05:48.910
1975. That was it. They all wore white shoes, looked like

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.050
they just came off the boat - nice press pants, all went to

00:05:53.069 --> 00:05:58.339
the same school - very small eco system. And not only was it

00:05:58.359 --> 00:06:02.970
3000 Santo Road. It was limited to a few regions - 3000

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:07.310
Santo Road, Miguel Park, California. You wanted money? That

00:06:07.329 --> 00:06:09.319
was the address.

00:06:09.339 --> 00:06:12.629
So, what's happened? What's happened is something kind of

00:06:12.649 --> 00:06:16.780
interesting. These were all constraints on eliminating the

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.610
number of startups certainly in Silicon Valley. I'll observe

00:06:20.629 --> 00:06:25.379
in the last five years almost all of these constraints

00:06:25.399 --> 00:06:29.810
certainly for the wave of internet commerce and social

00:06:29.829 --> 00:06:33.129
networking companies we've seen have been eliminated all at

00:06:33.149 --> 00:06:38.120
once. Let me tell you why.

00:06:38.139 --> 00:06:41.470
Startup constraints have been replaced by what I call an

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:45.560
entrepreneurial explosion. Now the cost of getting to a

00:06:45.579 --> 00:06:49.790
first product can be developed if you're doing a software

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:55.930
company on inexpensive PC's, Macs, using open source

00:06:55.949 --> 00:07:01.560
software. The cost of starting a company is no longer

00:07:01.579 --> 00:07:03.240
measured in millions; it's measured in your credit card

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:04.240
limit.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:10.550
A Short Time for First Product

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:13.839
It no longer takes years to develop a product. Using an

00:07:13.859 --> 00:07:17.589
agile methodology - that is a software methodology that

00:07:17.609 --> 00:07:22.000
allows you to develop incrementally and

00:07:22.019 --> 00:07:24.290
[inaudible] - now says you could get a minimum viable

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:28.560
product or a core part of your product out the door in weeks

00:07:28.579 --> 00:07:32.350
if not months or months if not weeks.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.620
Fast Customer Adoption

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:38.079
Our customers are no longer only the government and

00:07:38.099 --> 00:07:42.189
corporate. Silicon Valley has now turned to consumer

00:07:42.209 --> 00:07:46.050
applications. Who would have ever thought Facebook would

00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:49.620
have a half a billion customers! That would have been

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:53.009
science fiction - science fiction! Think about it. A Silicon

00:07:53.029 --> 00:07:57.409
Valley company with a half a billion customers in how many

00:07:57.429 --> 00:08:01.170
years? Three? Four? Unbelievable!

00:08:01.189 --> 00:08:04.540
Lower Startup Failure Rate

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.889
Startups until the last five or 10 years have been in hit or

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:13.360
miss proposition. We talked about the people, we talked

00:08:13.379 --> 00:08:16.399
about having great board members, but we really didn't

00:08:16.419 --> 00:08:22.579
understand the first line. Big insight in the last couple of

00:08:22.599 --> 00:08:26.730
years is no, startups are not smaller versions of large

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:31.409
companies. Probably if you take in anything from this talk,

00:08:31.429 --> 00:08:35.439
that's it. Everything you are - and how many of you are in

00:08:35.459 --> 00:08:38.830
Stanford GSB - in the Business School? Great! Everything you

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:44.100
learn in the Business School about large company management

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:50.379
is destructive for an early stage venture. Big idea.

00:08:50.399 --> 00:08:54.059
It's not kind of useful or "gee, I'll just change it". What

00:08:54.079 --> 00:08:58.649
you'll hear later and I'll explain why - it's destructive.

00:08:58.669 --> 00:09:01.939
Good news is in the entrepreneurial group at GSB we teach

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:05.009
the right things about early stage ventures, but they are

00:09:05.029 --> 00:09:08.029
not interchangeable. And you'll hear me say this throughout

00:09:08.049 --> 00:09:13.439
this talk. Large corporations execute existing business

00:09:13.459 --> 00:09:20.259
models. Startups search for them. The tools needed,

00:09:20.279 --> 00:09:27.399
techniques needed, the people needed for execution vs.

00:09:27.419 --> 00:09:31.799
search is what we now know to be radically different.

00:09:31.819 --> 00:09:35.500
Understanding those rules is what the rest of this talk will

00:09:35.519 --> 00:09:36.649
be about.

00:09:36.669 --> 00:09:40.309
But let me just finish the circle. We now have a much larger

00:09:40.329 --> 00:09:44.649
pool of risk capital just in Silicon Valley. I couldn't even

00:09:44.669 --> 00:09:47.559
put all the names on here. There's been an explosion in the

00:09:47.559 --> 00:09:51.889
last 20 years and even more so in the last five or 10 of a

00:09:51.909 --> 00:10:00.220
pool of available capital. Seed Rounds, Super Angels -

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.639
larger pools of venture capital all have kind of exploded.

00:10:04.659 --> 00:10:10.840
And venture capital and entrepreneurship is limited to

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:14.669
technology clusters like Silicon Valley. We can now easily

00:10:14.689 --> 00:10:18.279
say it's just as easy to start a company that's in Miguel

00:10:18.299 --> 00:10:21.049
Park as it is in San Francisco, New York, Shanghai, Israel,

00:10:21.049 --> 00:10:24.769
Chile, Singapore, Finland, etc. while still the highest

00:10:24.789 --> 00:10:29.879
concentration of technology innovation in the U.S. is here

00:10:29.899 --> 00:10:33.759
because basically we're a company town meaning our cluster

00:10:33.779 --> 00:10:37.620
does nothing but this. There are multiple clusters that are

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:41.830
forming in other cities. So, this talk is going to be about

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:47.250
one of those pieces of the explosion, about what we now know

00:10:47.269 --> 00:10:51.990
about how to lower startup failure rate and that's the

00:10:52.009 --> 00:10:55.850
purpose of the talk.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:11:00.730
The next story about that starts with this notion that not

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:05.750
all startups are equal. Now, I've been in Silicon Valley for

00:11:05.769 --> 00:11:08.949
more decades than I care to remember or are actually

00:11:08.969 --> 00:11:13.759
possible to remember by now. But one of the things that

00:11:13.779 --> 00:11:17.960
surprised me teaching at Stanford was going into a classroom

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.870
of some other professor and hearing them talk about

00:11:20.889 --> 00:11:24.129
entrepreneurship and who an entrepreneur was. And with the

00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:29.429
shock of me not recognizing who they were describing because

00:11:29.449 --> 00:11:32.539
it turns out if you get three professors in the room to try

00:11:32.539 --> 00:11:35.360
to define startups and entrepreneurship, you'll get nine

00:11:35.379 --> 00:11:39.860
definitions including I throw four of them in myself.

00:11:39.879 --> 00:11:44.090
It dawned on me that we really lack the common language of "

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:46.789
Who's an entrepreneur? What's a startup?" Even more

00:11:46.809 --> 00:11:49.669
important now that our government has decided to get

00:11:49.689 --> 00:11:54.340
involved with entrepreneurship and startups. In fact, I tend

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:57.679
to panic hearing that the Labor Department is going to have

00:11:57.699 --> 00:12:01.230
an occupational code for entrepreneur. We should all figure

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.019
that it's about time that entrepreneurship has jumped the

00:12:04.039 --> 00:12:05.949
shark.

00:12:05.969 --> 00:12:09.029
But I want to give you my definition of the types of

00:12:09.049 --> 00:12:14.100
entrepreneurs there are. You know, there are small

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.090
businesses in the United States that are entrepreneurial. My

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:23.580
parents came to the United States as immigrants in

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.779
[inaudible] bottom of a boat. Passed under the Statue of

00:12:26.799 --> 00:12:29.299
Liberty, came through Ellis Island, lived on the lower east

00:12:29.299 --> 00:12:33.139
side of New York, and their dream was to open their own

00:12:33.159 --> 00:12:36.899
grocery store. That was their dream, American Dream - be

00:12:36.919 --> 00:12:39.870
self - employed, not work in the garment district, you know,

00:12:39.889 --> 00:12:42.470
be able to do what they wanted to do.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.379
Ten years later they managed to scrimp and save enough money

00:12:45.399 --> 00:12:49.460
that they did. And their job was to serve known customers

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.860
with a known product and feed the family. And their exit

00:12:52.879 --> 00:12:56.000
criteria for them being a startup was they were finding a

00:12:56.019 --> 00:12:58.789
business model. Their business model consisted of

00:12:58.809 --> 00:13:02.500
understanding the types of foods their customers wanted,

00:13:02.519 --> 00:13:06.389
stocking them, and figuring out how to price them. And they

00:13:06.409 --> 00:13:09.779
did it with an existing team - my mother and father, and

00:13:09.799 --> 00:13:12.679
then when we were old enough to see over the pickle barrel

00:13:12.679 --> 00:13:16.799
it was us. And if they made $1 million in their entire

00:13:16.819 --> 00:13:21.909
lifetime would have been a lot of money.

00:13:21.929 --> 00:13:24.980
But I believe - and still believe - that my parents were

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.419
entrepreneurs. And they did do a startup, but their goals

00:13:30.439 --> 00:13:33.590
were very different from what we consider startups in

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:37.509
Silicon Valley. Their goal of a startup was to be self -

00:13:37.529 --> 00:13:41.120
employed, not have anybody tell them what to do, and they

00:13:41.139 --> 00:13:43.329
did not wake up in the morning thinking they were going to

00:13:43.329 --> 00:13:46.449
take over the grocery business in the United States. Their

00:13:46.469 --> 00:13:51.710
vision was limited and they were happy to be limited.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.099
But at the same time, there was no way they were going to be

00:13:55.099 --> 00:13:58.099
able to go to a venture capitalist - even if one existed at

00:13:58.099 --> 00:14:00.799
the time - and raise money. Money came from bank loans,

00:14:00.819 --> 00:14:03.960
friends and family, whatever they could save out of profit.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:09.649
So, I just want to observe that small business startups are

00:14:09.669 --> 00:14:15.529
still the core of what America does. There are 5.7 million

00:14:15.549 --> 00:14:20.409
small businesses in the United States today. It's 99.7% of

00:14:20.429 --> 00:14:26.230
all registered companies. It employs 50% of all non -

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:30.870
governmental workers are in small business startups.

00:14:30.889 --> 00:14:34.059
Now what's funny, in Silicon Valley we have a term for these

00:14:34.059 --> 00:14:39.990
people. We call them lifestyle businesses. And some of you

00:14:40.009 --> 00:14:42.509
are like, "Yeah! I'll be in the lifestyle business." Right?

00:14:42.509 --> 00:14:45.549
They could be people who are consultants, people who run

00:14:45.569 --> 00:14:48.699
their own, you known, grocery store, hairdresser,

00:14:48.719 --> 00:14:53.139
carpenters, farmers, small groups of plumbers, all are

00:14:53.159 --> 00:14:56.289
lifestyle businesses. You know what the real name for these

00:14:56.309 --> 00:15:01.069
people are? Normal. Right?

00:15:01.089 --> 00:15:06.819
Those of you sitting in this room are the ones who are crazy

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:11.169
because what these people want to do is live a life and then

00:15:11.189 --> 00:15:13.879
go out - if they have some spare time - and actually enjoy

00:15:13.879 --> 00:15:19.720
it. Their goal is not to work 24/7 though some of them get

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.500
trapped in doing that. But their goal is not to take over

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.340
the universe or anything else, but they are entrepreneurs.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:30.000
I will observe that there's another group of entrepreneurs

00:15:30.019 --> 00:15:32.809
and startups. And we also - if we're Silicon Valley

00:15:32.829 --> 00:15:37.649
entrepreneurs - we tend to diss these people too. It's

00:15:37.669 --> 00:15:41.740
entrepreneurship in large corporations. Fifteen years ago a

00:15:41.759 --> 00:15:45.009
professor at Harvard named Clayton Christianson really

00:15:45.029 --> 00:15:48.669
nailed this definition. Christianson said, "Look, there are

00:15:48.689 --> 00:15:52.769
two types of innovation in a large corporation. One is

00:15:52.789 --> 00:15:56.740
sustaining innovation. That is large companies start with a

00:15:56.759 --> 00:16:02.610
core product and as they mature they start spinning out

00:16:02.629 --> 00:16:06.959
innovations around that core - new features, different

00:16:06.979 --> 00:16:12.559
pricing, add on's, supplementary products - and large

00:16:12.579 --> 00:16:16.959
companies as a whole tend to be good at this. Sustaining

00:16:16.979 --> 00:16:19.569
innovation deals with existing products and known customers

00:16:19.589 --> 00:16:22.429
with known product features.

00:16:22.449 --> 00:16:26.079
But large companies have a finite life because of things

00:16:26.099 --> 00:16:29.319
that happen mostly out of their control - competitors

00:16:29.339 --> 00:16:35.100
emerge, new technologies show up, customer tastes change,

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:40.299
legislative or legal changes - all the sudden large

00:16:40.319 --> 00:16:44.639
companies are faced with disruptive innovation. And

00:16:44.659 --> 00:16:48.939
disruptive innovation requires them to come up with new

00:16:48.959 --> 00:16:52.829
products, new technology, deal with new customers, new

00:16:52.849 --> 00:16:54.019
markets and new channels.

00:16:54.039 --> 00:16:59.889
The best example closest to home is Google, the world's

00:16:59.909 --> 00:17:04.009
leader in search. They were going to own the planet forever

00:17:04.029 --> 00:17:07.569
until somebody raised the pirate flag in Palo Alto - a bunch

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:13.910
of guys called Facebook. Holy cow! Google which was the

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:18.799
world's leader in your interaction with a browser literally

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:22.960
in a span of three years found a competitor growing up in a

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:28.299
domain which may have absolutely no expertise. Google is

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:32.649
facing disruptive innovation and will do anything they need

00:17:32.669 --> 00:17:34.889
to do because they're a smart and innovative company

00:17:34.889 --> 00:17:39.960
including acquire IP, technology, product, companies, etc.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.369
So, go deal with this threat.

00:17:42.389 --> 00:17:47.769
But I just want to point out that inside of large companies

00:17:47.789 --> 00:17:51.629
there are groups that are equivalent to startups today who

00:17:51.649 --> 00:17:58.389
are searching for a business model, not executing one. By

00:17:58.409 --> 00:18:02.980
the way, the way you deal with disruptive innovation in a

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.980
large company is 1) you try to build it.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.669
That is "Ah great! There is Facebook. We'll build Facebook

00:18:08.669 --> 00:18:12.839
inside. Look how big we are. We can do that." And I don't

00:18:12.859 --> 00:18:15.609
know if any of you ever wondered, but I always wondered, "

00:18:15.629 --> 00:18:21.200
How come large companies get tripped by the underwear at

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:24.129
their ankles every time they try to compete?" They've got

00:18:24.149 --> 00:18:27.149
like a gazillion resources! How come they can't compete with

00:18:27.169 --> 00:18:31.200
scrappy little startups? And the answer is Shakespearean and

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:34.789
it's tragic. And we'll be talking about this in the rest of

00:18:34.809 --> 00:18:35.849
the talk.

00:18:35.869 --> 00:18:40.200
Large companies - once they get to a certain size - hire

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:44.569
people to execute known business models. They manage those

00:18:44.589 --> 00:18:53.579
people on profitability and success in executing the model

00:18:53.599 --> 00:18:58.799
they were hired for. Large corporations don't hire

00:18:58.819 --> 00:19:02.980
generically innovative people and I don't mean they don't

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.069
have innovative people.

00:19:04.089 --> 00:19:08.819
I'll give you an example - McDonald's. Anyone want to admit

00:19:08.839 --> 00:19:11.089
ever walking into a McDonald's? Okay. Good. Alright.

00:19:11.089 --> 00:19:16.990
McDonald's in Des Moines, Iowa and McDonald's in Miguel

00:19:17.009 --> 00:19:20.450
Park, California believe it or not have essentially the same

00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:22.069
menu.

00:19:22.089 --> 00:19:26.480
Imagine one day the Miguel Park McDonald's hires an out of

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:30.039
work entrepreneur as the manager by accident - it's going to

00:19:30.059 --> 00:19:34.670
happen one day - who wakes up and says, "This menu's boring.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:38.519
You know, we ought to be serving pizza." The next morning

00:19:38.539 --> 00:19:41.799
there's a line almost rioting outside the McDonald's because

00:19:41.819 --> 00:19:43.569
you can't get your Big Mac anymore.

00:19:43.569 --> 00:19:49.000
The point is McDonald's doesn't want innovators in the

00:19:49.019 --> 00:19:52.759
majority of its execution change. Somewhere back in Illinois

00:19:52.779 --> 00:19:56.899
I'm sure they have a lab of testing innovative ideas, but

00:19:56.919 --> 00:20:01.740
99.7% of employees in a large corporation are hired to

00:20:01.759 --> 00:20:05.980
execute an existing business model which makes trying to do

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:11.799
disruptive innovation internally 10 x or 100 x harder, not

00:20:11.819 --> 00:20:15.539
cause people are stupid in large corporations - far from it!

00:20:15.559 --> 00:20:18.769
But they were actually hired to go do one thing and their

00:20:18.789 --> 00:20:22.069
incentive to do one thing. And here are these crazy people

00:20:22.089 --> 00:20:24.929
in the offices next door to them trying to say, "We're not

00:20:24.929 --> 00:20:27.690
filling out the expense reports in triplicate. We're

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:32.079
entrepreneurs!" Just culture clash all the time.

00:20:32.099 --> 00:20:36.649
So, large companies tend to acquire - not always, but often

00:20:36.669 --> 00:20:39.940
- they acquire intellectual property. They might acquire

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:43.420
talent, head count - which is by the way why Google acquired

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:49.349
50 companies last year, most of it for people. They inquire

00:20:49.369 --> 00:20:52.509
product lines, or they acquire for customers, or they

00:20:52.529 --> 00:20:56.500
inquire for entire businesses and P &amp; L's - various reasons

00:20:56.519 --> 00:20:57.519
why companies acquire.

00:20:57.519 --> 00:21:04.379
So, I just want to point out so far we have two types of

00:21:04.399 --> 00:21:09.809
innovators, two types of startups - small business, large

00:21:09.829 --> 00:21:13.250
companies. But that's not what Silicon Valley is about.

00:21:13.269 --> 00:21:18.799
Silicon Valley is about this - a third group which I call

00:21:18.819 --> 00:21:22.629
not startups, but scalable startups just for the sake of

00:21:22.649 --> 00:21:28.039
making the point that you do a startup in Silicon Valley, it

00:21:28.059 --> 00:21:33.599
is not "I want to grow from 1 - 5 people." It's "I want to

00:21:33.619 --> 00:21:40.710
do something substantive. I want to change something. I want

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:44.269
to build something important." And typically the goal is to

00:21:44.289 --> 00:21:47.730
solve for unknown customers and unknown features. And you

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:52.129
start as a startup on the left, but your goal is to find a

00:21:52.149 --> 00:21:55.549
business model, and to grow into a large market, and to grow

00:21:55.569 --> 00:21:58.639
into a large company.

00:21:58.659 --> 00:22:03.359
Startups search for repeatable and scalable business models.

00:22:03.379 --> 00:22:07.389
Your goal is to go from the box on the left to be a box on

00:22:07.409 --> 00:22:12.309
the right - a company that could execute. A scalable startup

00:22:12.329 --> 00:22:16.539
is designed to grow big and what differentiates it as well

00:22:16.559 --> 00:22:19.480
from a small business in a large corporation is that it

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:24.269
typically needs risk capital - venture capital. By the way,

00:22:24.289 --> 00:22:26.389
anybody know what venture capital used to be called?

00:22:26.389 --> 00:22:30.119
In the 1950's when they came up with the name they said, "Oh

00:22:30.139 --> 00:22:32.500
yeah! Why don't we call it 'Adventure Capital'?" And the

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:36.549
bankers in New York said, "No". And they said, "Okay. We'll

00:22:36.569 --> 00:22:41.589
shorten it." So, Venture Capital. But when Silicon Valley

00:22:41.609 --> 00:22:44.750
says startup what we're really talking about is not small

00:22:44.769 --> 00:22:47.899
business startups and not large corporation startups. Does

00:22:47.919 --> 00:22:51.059
this make sense?

00:22:51.079 --> 00:22:53.950
Why it's really important is why we sit here in Silicon

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:57.909
Valley we all think we kind of know what the word needs. You

00:22:57.929 --> 00:23:01.789
step out of this bubble, go to Washington, D.C. and try to

00:23:01.809 --> 00:23:05.470
talk to legislature or the executive branch. Go to

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:11.480
Sacramento. Go to Nebraska. People have no idea what this

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:15.169
is. They know what small business entrepreneurship is.

00:23:15.189 --> 00:23:18.419
Making sure we use the right words and also as an educator

00:23:18.439 --> 00:23:22.720
making sure we educate correctly - Outside of the United

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:27.200
States this is the unknown. Outside of the U.S. small

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:29.730
business entrepreneurship is what people think entrepreneurs

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:31.079
do.

00:23:31.099 --> 00:23:35.869
So, here's my definition of what a Silicon Valley startups

00:23:35.889 --> 00:23:41.930
is - which by the way, just help me understand what is it

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:47.619
we're supposed to be doing? My definition is that a startup'

00:23:47.639 --> 00:23:52.389
s is a temporary organization used to search for a scalable

00:23:52.409 --> 00:23:56.230
and repeatable business model. That's a big mouthful, but

00:23:56.250 --> 00:24:02.259
pretty important. One is a temporary organization.

00:24:02.279 --> 00:24:06.599
My belief? There's no such thing as a 12 year old startup.

00:24:06.619 --> 00:24:09.049
There's a two year old startup attached to a 10 year old

00:24:09.049 --> 00:24:15.970
failure. Big idea because your job in a startup is to

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:20.769
search. If you knew exactly how to execute you just would

00:24:20.789 --> 00:24:25.339
hire the first 150 people as quick as you can. You actually

00:24:25.359 --> 00:24:29.210
are looking for something and what you're looking for is a

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:32.859
repeatable and scalable business model. The words "

00:24:32.879 --> 00:24:35.250
repeatable" and "scalable" should make sense, but the words

00:24:35.269 --> 00:24:38.789
"business model" I will also define as we get further into

00:24:38.809 --> 00:24:40.669
this talk. Make sense so far?

00:24:40.689 --> 00:24:47.529
So by the way, have any of you been in startups? Yeah. So,

00:24:47.549 --> 00:24:50.490
there's a secret memo none of you have ever gotten. Any of

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:53.750
you venture capitalists? No one's going to admit it. Okay.

00:24:53.769 --> 00:24:58.349
There's a secret VC memo because instead of this diagram you

00:24:58.369 --> 00:25:01.779
go from startup to large company. VC's actually know it

00:25:01.799 --> 00:25:07.179
doesn't work like that. You go from startup to transition to

00:25:07.199 --> 00:25:08.199
a large company.

00:25:08.199 --> 00:25:14.919
I always thought, "Gee! Okay! I get it! I leave being a

00:25:14.939 --> 00:25:17.889
startup when I find a scalable, repeatable business model

00:25:17.909 --> 00:25:21.079
and when I do that, my expectation was at the board meeting

00:25:21.099 --> 00:25:25.980
at the minimum they were going to pin a medal on my shirt,

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.519
give me a handshake - maybe a gold watch - and parade me,

00:25:28.519 --> 00:25:31.589
you know, up and down the aisles of the company with

00:25:31.609 --> 00:25:34.129
confetti saying, "Congratulations".

00:25:34.149 --> 00:25:36.389
Instead, you know what happens to most entrepreneurs when

00:25:36.389 --> 00:25:40.500
they find a scalable, repeatable model? Board meeting kind

00:25:40.519 --> 00:25:47.769
of goes like this. The VC's for the first time - first time

00:25:47.789 --> 00:25:51.250
- VC's who didn't even know your name are now paying

00:25:51.269 --> 00:25:54.589
attention to you. I don't know if all you know, but a

00:25:54.609 --> 00:25:57.460
typical Silicon Valley PC sits on anywhere from 8 - 12

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:00.440
boards, right? So, when they're physically showing up at

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:02.929
your board meeting they physically have to get out their

00:26:02.929 --> 00:26:06.490
Blackberry, or Android phone, or iPhone and look up your

00:26:06.509 --> 00:26:09.470
name, right? Then they're looking at the sign on the

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:12.759
building to figure out they're at the right company. You see

00:26:12.779 --> 00:26:16.240
one VC. They see 12 CEO's in a month.

00:26:16.259 --> 00:26:19.189
And by the way, when you're talking about your board meeting

00:26:19.189 --> 00:26:21.539
"Blah. Blah. Blah. Blah. Blah. Blah. Blah. Blah. Searching

00:26:21.559 --> 00:26:24.109
blah. Blah. Blah." You know what? They're kind of listening.

00:26:24.109 --> 00:26:27.579
They might be on their phone, but the board meeting when you

00:26:27.599 --> 00:26:32.279
say, "And we think we've cracked the code and we have some

00:26:32.299 --> 00:26:35.259
repeatable revenue where users" or something - all the

00:26:35.279 --> 00:26:40.259
sudden you've got that VC's attention because for the first

00:26:40.279 --> 00:26:45.669
time you're no longer a faceless mass in his portfolio. But

00:26:45.689 --> 00:26:49.089
all the sudden, you're an important company that might

00:26:49.109 --> 00:26:55.250
actually provide a substantive return in that VC's return.

00:26:55.269 --> 00:27:01.750
All the sudden, the VC is now looking at you in a way that's

00:27:01.769 --> 00:27:05.119
making you kind of uncomfortable because they're looking at

00:27:05.139 --> 00:27:09.509
you up, and down, and through the side. And what they're

00:27:09.529 --> 00:27:13.759
actually thinking is, "Okay. You got me out of the box on

00:27:13.779 --> 00:27:18.599
the left. But are you the person capable of taking me to the

00:27:18.619 --> 00:27:20.879
box on the right?"

00:27:20.899 --> 00:27:24.519
You did great flying around the country. You did great, you

00:27:24.539 --> 00:27:27.839
know, doing one thing on Tuesday, the next on Wednesday, and

00:27:27.859 --> 00:27:30.789
something different, and hiring and firing, or whatever. But

00:27:30.809 --> 00:27:34.619
gee, you've just told me we no longer need to do that. What

00:27:34.639 --> 00:27:38.470
you've just told me is we've gone from search where we're

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:42.690
doing different things every day to repeatable where we need

00:27:42.710 --> 00:27:48.309
to execute. Now, my question is as the VC is are you the guy

00:27:48.329 --> 00:27:52.819
or the woman? Interesting question.

00:27:52.839 --> 00:27:56.549
So, there's really a box in the middle called the transition

00:27:56.569 --> 00:27:59.230
and what the VC's don't tell you is you're going from

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:03.819
search, to build, to execute. And what happens in this build

00:28:03.839 --> 00:28:11.179
phase is most often then not the founders depart. And that's

00:28:11.199 --> 00:28:14.529
a very polite way of saying you're holding onto the

00:28:14.549 --> 00:28:18.929
doorframe and the VC's are pushing you out because most

00:28:18.949 --> 00:28:22.759
first timers - even often second or third time CEO's - don't

00:28:22.779 --> 00:28:26.319
quite understand it's not personal. But right here your

00:28:26.339 --> 00:28:30.299
interest and your VC's interest which were aligned are now

00:28:30.319 --> 00:28:34.139
diverged because VC's have a different business model than

00:28:34.159 --> 00:28:37.589
you and their career objective is different than you. It

00:28:37.609 --> 00:28:40.679
isn't personal, it's business.

00:28:40.699 --> 00:28:45.659
And the business part is - what venture capitalists want to

00:28:45.679 --> 00:28:52.179
see - is somebody capable of bringing in scale that is

00:28:52.199 --> 00:28:56.559
growing your startup to a large company. Are you as the

00:28:56.579 --> 00:29:01.269
founder capable of doing that? And more often than not, they

00:29:01.289 --> 00:29:04.889
bring in what's majorly called "the suit" but truly is an

00:29:04.909 --> 00:29:08.210
operator - somebody who's an operating executive who is

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:11.789
actually skilled at doing this. Make sense so far? Anybody

00:29:11.809 --> 00:29:14.679
ever encounter this? This is why. It's a secret memo you

00:29:14.679 --> 00:29:17.189
never get and it's nothing personal.

00:29:17.209 --> 00:29:21.439
The bad news is - and I laugh about this with my friends who

00:29:21.459 --> 00:29:25.279
are VC's - how come you don't tell founders this? And they

00:29:25.299 --> 00:29:29.309
go, "Steve, so imagine I'd be the only VC telling you, 'Hey,

00:29:29.329 --> 00:29:32.730
by the way, when you succeed I'm going to replace you.' Do

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:35.569
you think I'd ever get another deal?" And so, this is kind

00:29:35.569 --> 00:29:39.839
of the secret. And it actually does injustice to

00:29:39.859 --> 00:29:42.799
entrepreneurs not understanding this is a normal part of the

00:29:42.819 --> 00:29:43.909
process.

00:29:43.929 --> 00:29:51.049
The interesting conundrum is think about who runs today the

00:29:51.069 --> 00:29:54.569
most successful technology companies in the world - Steve

00:29:54.589 --> 00:30:00.939
Jobs, Lori Allison, Jeff Bezos, at least for the first

00:30:00.959 --> 00:30:04.109
couple of decades Bill Gates. I'll contend they're the

00:30:04.129 --> 00:30:07.990
exceptions to prove the rule. And by the way, remember Jobs

00:30:08.009 --> 00:30:12.069
had to get fired by an operating executive he brought in to

00:30:12.089 --> 00:30:15.189
go understand this. There's a growth process that most

00:30:15.209 --> 00:30:19.409
founders don't actually go through fast enough. World class

00:30:19.429 --> 00:30:23.429
founders actually figure out how to quickly - almost like a

00:30:23.449 --> 00:30:28.859
chameleon - adapt and learn a set of skills that most of us

00:30:28.879 --> 00:30:31.799
take multiple companies to go do.

00:30:31.819 --> 00:30:34.549
By the way, anybody know how many presidents Bill Gates went

00:30:34.549 --> 00:30:38.109
through at Microsoft? I think it was at least five and he

00:30:38.129 --> 00:30:41.629
learned from every one of them - every one of them. That

00:30:41.649 --> 00:30:45.409
make sense? Just keep that in mind. By the way - as an aside

00:30:45.429 --> 00:30:48.240
- as I said, if you fail you stay a startup.

00:30:48.259 --> 00:30:52.250
So, one of the things I thought I'd share is my startups

00:30:52.269 --> 00:30:56.779
aren't small versions of a large company. And this really is

00:30:56.799 --> 00:31:01.200
an interesting thing to process cause when I started in the

00:31:01.220 --> 00:31:04.139
Silicon Valley I said, "Oh yeah! I know what we do! We do

00:31:04.139 --> 00:31:06.659
everything that we used to do in our large company. We'll do

00:31:06.659 --> 00:31:10.740
it here in our startup." So, I'll suggest that that's very

00:31:10.759 --> 00:31:15.509
different. I'll suggest that's wrong. That in a startup -

00:31:15.529 --> 00:31:18.689
the box on the left - we're searching for a business model

00:31:18.709 --> 00:31:21.710
and it needs to be found by the founders.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:25.589
Now, we're looking for product/market fit and we're looking

00:31:25.609 --> 00:31:30.129
for a repeatable sales market, and finally, when we exit the

00:31:30.149 --> 00:31:33.769
startup phase we can start hiring managers. But in a large

00:31:33.789 --> 00:31:37.899
company you're executing and you become a large company when

00:31:37.919 --> 00:31:40.199
you kind of hit cash flow, you break even, and you're

00:31:40.199 --> 00:31:42.449
profitable, and you're hitting a rapid scale, and you're

00:31:42.449 --> 00:31:44.509
hiring new senior management. That's something called

00:31:44.529 --> 00:31:47.929
Dunbar's Rule. About 150 people, that's another major

00:31:47.949 --> 00:31:49.000
organizational shift.

00:31:49.019 --> 00:31:52.980
Anybody know why 150 people become a company? Anybody know

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:57.519
Dunbar's Rule? It turns out that human beings can't remember

00:31:57.539 --> 00:32:00.789
more than 150 people. It's not exactly that number, but plus

00:32:00.809 --> 00:32:06.559
or minus. You're just as a human being. We're hard wired for

00:32:06.579 --> 00:32:10.319
that number. You've become a company and when you've become

00:32:10.339 --> 00:32:15.220
a company, your drive for profitability is hiring people who

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:18.039
know how to do the same thing cause you've already figured

00:32:18.039 --> 00:32:19.819
out what that successful model is.

00:32:19.839 --> 00:32:25.769
Let me give you another difference. Large corporations run

00:32:25.789 --> 00:32:28.649
on balance sheets, cash flows, and income statements.

00:32:28.669 --> 00:32:31.539
Anybody in the GSB taking an accounting class? Is it a

00:32:31.559 --> 00:32:36.379
required class? Right. If you're running a company and you

00:32:36.399 --> 00:32:40.089
don't know how to do corporate accounting you're going to

00:32:40.109 --> 00:32:42.859
put the company out of business I don't care what department

00:32:42.859 --> 00:32:45.939
you're in. You need to know that. It's the very basis of how

00:32:45.939 --> 00:32:47.649
we manage and grow companies.

00:32:47.669 --> 00:32:54.189
Anybody ever been in a startup board meeting? Now, I've got

00:32:54.209 --> 00:32:57.369
to tell you I was in startup board meetings for eight

00:32:57.389 --> 00:33:01.409
startups, 20 years. First board meeting, "Alright what do we

00:33:01.429 --> 00:33:05.039
do?" "What we do is we show them balance sheet, income

00:33:05.059 --> 00:33:08.730
statement, cash flow." "Okay. Great. What's the income

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:12.849
statement say month zero - month one?" "Zero." "What's it

00:33:12.869 --> 00:33:17.379
say month two?" "Zero." "Month three?" "Zero."

00:33:17.399 --> 00:33:20.789
But not only am I paying somebody to prepare all these

00:33:20.809 --> 00:33:24.309
documents, we actually think that the Board is actually

00:33:24.329 --> 00:33:28.699
managing something by looking at these numbers here on the

00:33:28.719 --> 00:33:33.529
left. I'll contend from now on if any board does that in a

00:33:33.549 --> 00:33:36.929
startup, you walk out of the room. The only thing you need

00:33:36.949 --> 00:33:40.289
to know about numbers in a startup in the early days - day

00:33:40.309 --> 00:33:46.659
one - that has to do with dollars is, "How much cash do you

00:33:46.679 --> 00:33:50.679
have left and what rate are you burning it at until you hit

00:33:50.699 --> 00:33:52.609
the ground?"

00:33:52.629 --> 00:33:55.689
There are other numbers that you need to know that are much

00:33:55.709 --> 00:33:58.500
more important that you want your board engaged with and

00:33:58.519 --> 00:34:02.529
that's the metrics associated with you trying to search for

00:34:02.549 --> 00:34:08.400
a business model. Here happens to be some for an internet

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:13.139
startup. Your own will be different. But the key idea is you

00:34:13.159 --> 00:34:16.610
want your board engaged in helping you figure out, "What

00:34:16.630 --> 00:34:19.799
numbers should we be looking at?" They ain't accounting

00:34:19.819 --> 00:34:23.429
numbers! You should be so lucky to have this problem as how

00:34:23.449 --> 00:34:26.929
to count the amount of cash we have or the amount of money

00:34:26.949 --> 00:34:30.230
that's coming in the door. And maybe you're the rare startup

00:34:30.250 --> 00:34:32.759
that actually needs to do that on day one.

00:34:32.779 --> 00:34:35.539
Most startups are trying to find a business model and you

00:34:35.559 --> 00:34:37.829
really want your board engaged in a much different

00:34:37.849 --> 00:34:40.989
conversation. "Are we looking at the right metrics?" "Are we

00:34:41.009 --> 00:34:43.089
going after the right people?" "Should we be measuring

00:34:43.089 --> 00:34:46.579
something else?" Make sense?

00:34:46.599 --> 00:34:48.409
Sales

00:34:48.429 --> 00:34:52.980
Anybody ever see a large corporate VP of Sales? Not large,

00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:56.690
but I mean a VP of Sales from a large corporation? Anybody

00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:59.840
ever see one? Huh? What do they look like? How tall are

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:05.969
they? Yeah, six feet or more. What color is their hair,

00:35:05.989 --> 00:35:12.190
Scott? Right, a little bit of gray. And what's their golf

00:35:12.210 --> 00:35:16.019
handicap? Single digits.

00:35:16.039 --> 00:35:19.809
Large corporation VP of Sales and we laugh about that? Those

00:35:19.829 --> 00:35:23.420
are actually good skills to have. Those are actually good

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:26.400
characteristics to have. They're also not stupid. They're

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:29.079
actually pretty smart, but they almost look in the United

00:35:29.099 --> 00:35:32.769
States like a type because they're world - class at building

00:35:32.789 --> 00:35:38.480
sales organizations, making those organizations scalable,

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:41.730
and having sales forces that could sell off our price lists,

00:35:41.750 --> 00:35:45.699
and data sheets, and they operate brilliantly to an existing

00:35:45.719 --> 00:35:51.219
revenue plan. That's what a world - class VP of Sales does.

00:35:51.239 --> 00:35:55.380
And when you do a startup, my dream was always, "Hey! I'll

00:35:55.400 --> 00:35:58.529
just hire one of those people and if I can't get one of

00:35:58.529 --> 00:36:01.279
those people I'll hire one of their directors and tell them

00:36:01.279 --> 00:36:04.309
they can be a VP in my startup." "Hey look! They did great

00:36:04.309 --> 00:36:05.309
over there!"

00:36:05.309 --> 00:36:11.750
I have to tell you my last startup, I hired a world - class

00:36:11.769 --> 00:36:15.819
VP of Sales early from Oracle and I still remember the day.

00:36:15.839 --> 00:36:20.559
We'd just got thrown out of our fourth company we'd called

00:36:20.579 --> 00:36:24.079
on in a row. It's pouring rain - kind of like a day like

00:36:24.099 --> 00:36:28.319
this but worse - and you know, I'm shell shocked as the

00:36:28.339 --> 00:36:31.829
founder. But I've got this great VP of Sales and he's

00:36:31.849 --> 00:36:34.849
driving. And he's about to start the car and I go, "Where

00:36:34.849 --> 00:36:37.599
are we going?" He said, "Well, we've got another call." I

00:36:37.599 --> 00:36:40.670
said, "Gee! Another call? We just got thrown out four times

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:44.650
in a row!" I said, "Don't we want to stop and think about

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:48.250
what we've learned?" He said, "Steve, great sales people

00:36:48.269 --> 00:36:51.829
just never take no, you know? They just keep going."

00:36:51.849 --> 00:36:56.420
And by the way, that's correct only when you actually

00:36:56.440 --> 00:37:01.679
understand a known model. When you don't know what the model

00:37:01.699 --> 00:37:07.619
is that's destructive behavior. And in fact, those people -

00:37:07.639 --> 00:37:12.119
world - class sales people - actually melt down in startups

00:37:12.139 --> 00:37:16.500
because this guy in our next week started freaking out when

00:37:16.519 --> 00:37:19.469
I said, "Well, this presentation isn't working. I'm going to

00:37:19.469 --> 00:37:23.279
make up a new one." And I was making it up on the drive to

00:37:23.299 --> 00:37:26.539
the customer. He goes, "You can't do that! I know what the

00:37:26.539 --> 00:37:30.900
existing pitch is!" "Jay, the existing pitch isn't working."

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:33.109
"Well yes, but that's the one we have." "Well, I'm the

00:37:33.109 --> 00:37:36.579
founder. We can make up a new one."

00:37:36.599 --> 00:37:39.969
Smoke would come out of his ears and it wasn't because he

00:37:39.989 --> 00:37:43.069
was done. It was actually because he was great at what we

00:37:43.089 --> 00:37:47.569
had hired him for and it was our mistake for not

00:37:47.589 --> 00:37:50.750
understanding the following:

00:37:50.769 --> 00:37:56.739
Titles on the right actually don't mean the same thing on

00:37:56.759 --> 00:38:03.429
the left. I'm sorry? I'm not sure I can, but I will try.

00:38:03.449 --> 00:38:08.360
Titles - VP of Sales, VP of Marketing, VP of Bus Dev - well

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:13.319
understood in a company that's executed. Gee, VP of Sales in

00:38:13.339 --> 00:38:16.259
a large corporation - hire the sales force, make the revenue

00:38:16.279 --> 00:38:20.019
plan. Hey! Execute the model, sell off the data sheet and

00:38:20.039 --> 00:38:23.579
price list, right? That's what a VP is. Takes whatever spec

00:38:23.599 --> 00:38:26.980
you want in a large corporation. That's pretty close.

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:31.210
In a startup, in the first year or two that's not what the

00:38:31.230 --> 00:38:35.659
job is. "Oh my God! What is the job?" "Well, I don't know.

00:38:35.679 --> 00:38:40.119
We're going to go get out of the building and figure it out.

00:38:40.139 --> 00:38:44.409
Data sheet, price list, website - we're going to change them

00:38:44.429 --> 00:38:48.759
until we get them right. If you don't have people capable of

00:38:48.779 --> 00:38:53.349
operating in chaos on the left, it explains why most of

00:38:53.369 --> 00:38:57.279
these people meltdown when they hit a startup because even

00:38:57.299 --> 00:39:01.699
though we use the same title, the job descriptions if we're

00:39:01.719 --> 00:39:04.730
actually honest are quite different. Does that make sense

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:06.750
now?

00:39:06.769 --> 00:39:12.440
Engineering same thing. Large corporation - great VP of

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:16.819
Engineering to take a market requirements document, put it

00:39:16.839 --> 00:39:21.989
together in a waterfall development plan, hire engineers and

00:39:22.009 --> 00:39:24.539
then at the end have a QA and Tech

00:39:24.559 --> 00:39:26.690
[inaudible]. I can always now tell when a startup is in

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:29.239
trouble because the first thing I ask is in the first year

00:39:29.239 --> 00:39:32.659
or two, "So, how many people do you have in QA and Tech

00:39:32.679 --> 00:39:37.630
[inaudible]." And if the answer is more than one it's a

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:42.480
disaster because in a startup in almost every case you need

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:46.940
to be doing a very different type of engineer. Waterfall in

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:50.469
a large development says, "I spec the product, and I

00:39:50.489 --> 00:39:52.019
execute, and you'll get it at the end."

00:39:52.019 --> 00:39:57.289
In a startup, except for corner cases you really don't know

00:39:57.309 --> 00:40:02.489
what to spec. And what you want to do is agile development,

00:40:02.509 --> 00:40:05.329
incremental and

00:40:05.349 --> 00:40:09.110
[inaudible] development. That requires much different

00:40:09.130 --> 00:40:14.920
engineering leadership and talent. So, we'll talk a little

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:17.449
bit later about the types of startups.

00:40:17.469 --> 00:40:20.690
There is a type of startup that's executing in an existing

00:40:20.710 --> 00:40:26.059
market. An existing market by my definition simply says, "

00:40:26.079 --> 00:40:29.980
You have a product which is substantively better as agreed

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.110
to by the existing customers in that market and you are

00:40:34.130 --> 00:40:37.059
going to take market share from the incumbent. If that's the

00:40:37.059 --> 00:40:40.349
product you have, then you could spec the entire product on

00:40:40.369 --> 00:40:44.750
day one. That's the corner case. Most any other type of

00:40:44.769 --> 00:40:47.449
product that's not the case. You're searching for what the

00:40:47.449 --> 00:40:50.069
right feature set should be. Does that make sense? And in

00:40:50.069 --> 00:40:52.400
fact, it's detailed in the book, "Four Steps of the

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:55.150
Epiphany". I'm not trying to flog the book, but just FYI.

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:59.150
Actual development, continuous deployment, continuous

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:02.860
learning, self - organizing teams and you're going to shift

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:06.549
not the maximum feature set - which is the goal in a large

00:41:06.569 --> 00:41:10.049
company, but the minimum feature set or sometimes called the

00:41:10.069 --> 00:41:15.259
minimum viable product - big idea. In a startup, you want to

00:41:15.279 --> 00:41:20.159
get to market as quick as possible, but not by offering

00:41:20.179 --> 00:41:23.989
every possible feature, by doing the exact opposite.

00:41:24.009 --> 00:41:28.980
Boy! That's really a hard concept. I want to offer the

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:32.389
minimum features on day 1. Whoever thought you could offer a

00:41:32.409 --> 00:41:35.360
smart phone without copy and paste? Anybody remember which

00:41:35.380 --> 00:41:40.500
smart phone that was? iPhone. Or who could ever offer a

00:41:40.519 --> 00:41:44.690
music player without an FM tuner? Anybody remember what that

00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:49.639
device was? iPod. Minimum feature setting, right, will

00:41:49.659 --> 00:41:53.900
incrementally add features later as needed. But first we

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:56.480
want to get the product out and see if our core idea is

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:57.639
correct.

00:41:57.659 --> 00:42:01.039
Last piece - especially for those of you in business school

00:42:01.059 --> 00:42:06.059
- large corporations love business plans. Business plans are

00:42:06.079 --> 00:42:09.940
wonderful in a large corporation. I know who the customers

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:13.429
are, I know who the market is, I'm doing a product line

00:42:13.449 --> 00:42:17.269
extension, I could spec everything and it describes a set of

00:42:17.289 --> 00:42:26.329
knowns. A business plan in a startup is a creative writing

00:42:26.349 --> 00:42:32.130
exercise. A business plan in a university I will contend is

00:42:32.150 --> 00:42:38.089
only done because it's easy to grade, not because it

00:42:38.109 --> 00:42:42.619
provides students any use at all. It's actually detrimental

00:42:42.639 --> 00:42:49.039
to your career. In a startup instead of a business plan, you

00:42:49.059 --> 00:42:51.529
want to spend your time working on a business model. And I

00:42:51.529 --> 00:42:54.170
will describe later what a business model is. But it

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:58.599
describes unknowns and sets you up for what you have to

00:42:58.619 --> 00:43:00.259
learn.

00:43:00.279 --> 00:43:04.690
So as an aside, how do startups search for a business model?

00:43:04.710 --> 00:43:07.730
Well, the search is something called customer development.

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:10.019
Here I've blown up that box at startup and the

00:43:10.019 --> 00:43:13.569
implementation is called agile development. And I'm going to

00:43:13.589 --> 00:43:18.429
talk a little bit more about customer development.

00:43:18.449 --> 00:43:23.239
After 40 years I think we understand why startups fail

00:43:23.259 --> 00:43:26.099
because more startups fail from a lack of customers than

00:43:26.119 --> 00:43:28.670
from a failure of product development. Now, this is a

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:32.639
mindblower in Silicon Valley. This goes across any

00:43:32.659 --> 00:43:35.690
technology except for biotech. We'll leave biotech aside.

00:43:35.710 --> 00:43:40.359
But hardware, software, Silicon - we take more technology

00:43:40.379 --> 00:43:44.019
risks here in Silicon Valley per square foot than any other

00:43:44.039 --> 00:43:48.079
place in the world. And you would think companies go out of

00:43:48.099 --> 00:43:51.119
business cause engineering screws it up.

00:43:51.139 --> 00:43:54.039
The reality is in most companies - in most markets - 90 -

00:43:54.059 --> 00:43:58.739
99% of the failures are from a lack of customers. Now, for

00:43:58.759 --> 00:44:01.469
those of you who are engineers don't feel smug because I've

00:44:01.469 --> 00:44:05.230
never seen a product delivered on time. But it's not like

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:08.309
the company goes out of business because of that. It goes

00:44:08.329 --> 00:44:09.829
out of business because of this.

00:44:09.829 --> 00:44:13.009
So, I find it very interesting that for the last 40 years

00:44:13.029 --> 00:44:16.679
Silicon Valley has had tons of processes to manage product

00:44:16.699 --> 00:44:21.009
development, but no processes to mange customer development

00:44:21.029 --> 00:44:24.719
- none. And in fact, the only thing we used to have is this

00:44:24.739 --> 00:44:27.659
product introduction model. Anybody ever see this before?

00:44:27.679 --> 00:44:28.679
You know,

00:44:28.679 --> 00:44:30.369
[inaudible] product development, alpha tests, beta tests,

00:44:30.369 --> 00:44:32.829
first customership. Anybody ever see this? Come on. Yep?

00:44:32.849 --> 00:44:36.750
In fact, when I used to put this up in my class students

00:44:36.769 --> 00:44:40.500
would say, "Yeah. Yeah." You know, "We've seen this." And I

00:44:40.519 --> 00:44:43.789
used to say, "Yeah. Even the waiters in San Francisco could

00:44:43.809 --> 00:44:46.969
draw this." And some of them used to say, "Well, we're

00:44:46.989 --> 00:44:49.989
waiters because we used to be dot com CEO's." And they said

00:44:49.989 --> 00:44:53.089
that so that I'd never make that joke again. So, I won't.

00:44:53.089 --> 00:45:03.009
But startups fail because of this diagram. This diagram says

00:45:03.029 --> 00:45:08.589
[inaudible] and convinces founders to make two major

00:45:08.609 --> 00:45:16.170
mistakes. One is, "I know what customers need and because I

00:45:16.190 --> 00:45:20.779
know what customers need, I could spec the product on day

00:45:20.799 --> 00:45:25.089
one. Right there." Now, this is pretty interesting because

00:45:25.109 --> 00:45:30.569
startups are an act of faith, right? They are driven by the

00:45:30.589 --> 00:45:35.109
passion of an entrepreneur. And so, part of your passion has

00:45:35.129 --> 00:45:39.480
to be a believe that you actually do know what problem to

00:45:39.500 --> 00:45:41.650
solve.

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:45.799
So, this is a real conundrum and the way I describe it is

00:45:45.819 --> 00:45:51.980
that on day one a startup is a faith - based exercise. Truly

00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:58.920
it's a passion. But the startups that survive very quickly

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:04.230
replace that faith with facts as quickly as possible. And

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:08.039
that is instead of executing completely on faith while I

00:46:08.059 --> 00:46:12.119
know it's my vision, they go, "I know it's my vision and I'm

00:46:12.139 --> 00:46:16.039
scared out of my whatever that I might be wrong." And so,

00:46:16.059 --> 00:46:18.489
here's the process to check it out. And that's what we're

00:46:18.489 --> 00:46:19.489
going to talk about.

00:46:19.489 --> 00:46:22.319
But first, I'm going to remind you how startups used to

00:46:22.319 --> 00:46:25.849
work. You'd draw this diagram in your board meeting, the

00:46:25.869 --> 00:46:29.299
board would node sagely yes and then you'd say, "Well, here'

00:46:29.319 --> 00:46:32.569
s my marketing plan about what I was going to do in what

00:46:32.569 --> 00:46:35.969
order. I'd create markup material, hire a PR agency, have it

00:46:35.989 --> 00:46:38.489
create demand, have a launch event". They'd nod and "Here's

00:46:38.489 --> 00:46:42.239
how I was going to hire my sales VP. I was going to hire a

00:46:42.239 --> 00:46:45.309
sales VP from a large company, build a sales organization in

00:46:45.329 --> 00:46:48.949
time for first customership, and then I'd hire the all

00:46:48.969 --> 00:46:53.000
important bus dev person, and then I'll hire engineering

00:46:53.019 --> 00:46:56.809
from day one. Maybe my co - founder will do all this stuff."

00:46:56.829 --> 00:47:00.159
This is how you go at a business. I think it's pretty well

00:47:00.159 --> 00:47:04.089
understood that what boards used to tell you was, "Yeah. You

00:47:04.109 --> 00:47:07.289
got it. Good. That's our plan." Now, it's actually the

00:47:07.309 --> 00:47:11.670
leading cause of death of a startup. Why? Because you really

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:14.629
don't find out whether you're right or wrong all the way

00:47:14.629 --> 00:47:18.380
until you've burned cash, hired a ton of people and launched

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:22.480
the product. So, in contrast here's how startups succeed.

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:26.109
They do something called customer development and this is

00:47:26.129 --> 00:47:30.259
where the founders get out of the building.

00:47:30.279 --> 00:47:32.449
Customer development says, "Instead of this product

00:47:32.469 --> 00:47:37.619
introduction model, we're going to replace it with something

00:47:37.639 --> 00:47:41.119
else. We're going to tell Engineering to go do agile

00:47:41.139 --> 00:47:45.619
development, but we're going to create a process for

00:47:45.639 --> 00:47:52.679
managing facts and that's a four step process. And that's

00:47:52.699 --> 00:47:54.929
called customer development."

00:47:54.949 --> 00:47:59.739
* This first step - customer discovery - gets you out of the

00:47:59.759 --> 00:48:03.849
building to test every hypothesis you have.

00:48:03.869 --> 00:48:09.460
* Customer validation ensures your hypotheses are correct by

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:13.429
actually getting users or orders that validate your

00:48:13.449 --> 00:48:16.049
solution.

00:48:16.069 --> 00:48:19.139
* Customer creation is where we scale the business.

00:48:19.159 --> 00:48:22.880
* And company building is that stage where I had labeled

00:48:22.900 --> 00:48:26.829
transition before - remember, where you get to leave? Maybe

00:48:26.849 --> 00:48:29.989
you don't get to leave anymore. You get to stay.

00:48:30.009 --> 00:48:32.049
And so, let's go through a bit of it.

00:48:32.049 --> 00:48:38.099
* Customer discovery says, "Listen, instead of writing a

00:48:38.119 --> 00:48:43.179
business plan, let's write down all the hypotheses. By the

00:48:43.199 --> 00:48:46.849
way, I use the hypotheses because does anybody know what

00:48:46.869 --> 00:48:51.119
current tuition is at GSB? What's current tuition? How much

00:48:51.139 --> 00:48:53.989
is it a year? $50,000, right?

00:48:54.009 --> 00:48:57.230
If I were just to say, "Test your business model guesses"

00:48:57.250 --> 00:49:00.559
you'd feel cheated, right? But since you're paying $50,000 a

00:49:00.579 --> 00:49:03.569
year inside a business school I use very expensive words,

00:49:03.589 --> 00:49:06.480
right? So, you can tell your parents, "I got my money's

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:09.349
worth." But if you really think about what's going on in a

00:49:09.349 --> 00:49:13.210
startup implicitly unless you're a domain expert - another

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:19.509
corner case - as a founder, you're actually guess. You're

00:49:19.529 --> 00:49:23.119
guessing and you're guessing about a lot of things or maybe

00:49:23.139 --> 00:49:25.469
you're guessing about a small set of them, but you're

00:49:25.469 --> 00:49:26.739
guessing.

00:49:26.759 --> 00:49:29.940
In customer discovery, we're going to say, "Guess what?

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:33.099
That's a normal part of startups. But we're going to make

00:49:33.099 --> 00:49:36.099
you write them down, and get out of the building, and start

00:49:36.099 --> 00:49:39.579
discovering whether they're true or not as quickly as

00:49:39.599 --> 00:49:44.500
possible in a formal process. And you're not allowed to

00:49:44.519 --> 00:49:53.420
outsource this. Why? Well, what if in fact you were a

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:54.440
founder?

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:57.429
You're a founder. Ready? Okay. Got a great product, your

00:49:57.449 --> 00:50:01.509
vision, and you hired a VP of Sales early on. And she comes

00:50:01.529 --> 00:50:07.639
back and says - what's your name? Nate? Nathan? Nate?

00:50:07.659 --> 00:50:10.389
Alright. Nate, let's be formal here. "Nathan, I'm your VP of

00:50:10.389 --> 00:50:13.579
Sales. You know, I got out early. I'm talking to these

00:50:13.599 --> 00:50:16.279
customers. You know what they say? The product sucks." What

00:50:16.299 --> 00:50:23.089
are you going to tell me? I'm your VP of Sales. Find better

00:50:23.109 --> 00:50:24.429
customers. Great!

00:50:24.449 --> 00:50:27.909
So, she comes back again after a month. "You know, I've

00:50:27.929 --> 00:50:32.329
flown all around the U.S. and I don't know how to say this.

00:50:32.349 --> 00:50:35.579
They think it really sucks." What do you say?

00:50:35.599 --> 00:50:40.219
[inaudible]. Right! And there may be a step in between that

00:50:40.239 --> 00:50:43.500
says, "You're just not explaining it correctly", right? Or "

00:50:43.519 --> 00:50:45.369
They just don't get it", right?

00:50:45.389 --> 00:50:48.199
And then you do exactly what Nathan said. You fire the VP of

00:50:48.199 --> 00:50:55.039
Sales. Is it going to get any better? But how come the CEO

00:50:55.059 --> 00:51:00.690
didn't say, "Well, maybe that's true." What's going on?

00:51:00.710 --> 00:51:05.929
What's going on? Why wouldn't you say, "Perhaps that's true"

00:51:05.949 --> 00:51:16.650
. Why? Why would you ignore facts? You think you're scared?

00:51:16.670 --> 00:51:17.670
[inaudible]

00:51:16.670 --> 00:51:18.670
Alright. It's an emotional investment whose consequence -

00:51:17.670 --> 00:51:18.670
yes?

00:51:18.670 --> 00:51:28.699
[inaudible]. Sometimes. Sometimes it's even worse than ego.

00:51:28.719 --> 00:51:34.670
By worse I mean you're so blind to your investment in not

00:51:34.690 --> 00:51:37.190
only time, money, but the idea that there's a cognizant

00:51:37.190 --> 00:51:41.159
dissidence that occurs. And by the way, this is a big idea.

00:51:41.179 --> 00:51:45.609
It will occur to every one of you in a startup. This is not

00:51:45.629 --> 00:51:49.750
anybody being stupid. This is human nature at work here.

00:51:49.769 --> 00:51:54.150
The only way to break this chain is the founders need to go

00:51:54.170 --> 00:51:59.559
out of the building. So Nathan, we're going to pick on you

00:51:59.579 --> 00:52:02.329
some more. You're still the CEO. You're out of the building

00:52:02.329 --> 00:52:05.579
and I'm a customer. And you ask me, "What do you think of

00:52:05.579 --> 00:52:06.579
the product? Ask me."

00:52:06.579 --> 00:52:08.079
Nathan: What do you think of the product?

00:52:08.079 --> 00:52:09.079
"It sucks."

00:52:09.079 --> 00:52:14.989
[inaudible]. "It still sucks, Nathan." What do you do now?

00:52:15.009 --> 00:52:17.509
You got to the next customer because you go, "Obviously

00:52:17.509 --> 00:52:19.009
they're an idiot. They're not deserving of my product."

00:52:19.009 --> 00:52:20.009
Right?

00:52:20.009 --> 00:52:24.529
Now, I would rather draw this out. It takes most people

00:52:24.549 --> 00:52:28.329
three times to hear it before smoke starts coming out of

00:52:28.349 --> 00:52:31.210
their own ears. By the way, it usually takes me about five

00:52:31.210 --> 00:52:34.460
or six times to hear it. But I'm going to say most people

00:52:34.460 --> 00:52:38.089
will never get it the first time. I guarantee you 99% of

00:52:38.109 --> 00:52:40.969
people won't get it the second time. It's kind of a bell

00:52:40.969 --> 00:52:44.599
curve. I was on the bleeding edge. In fact, what's worse is

00:52:44.619 --> 00:52:47.929
the greater your reality distortion field personally, the

00:52:47.949 --> 00:52:53.829
harder it is for you to listen. Big idea.

00:52:53.849 --> 00:52:55.980
By the way, if anybody's thinking of doing customer

00:52:55.980 --> 00:52:59.980
development, don't do it if it ain't you or your co -

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.389
founders getting out of the building. It can't be outsourced

00:53:03.409 --> 00:53:06.519
because you will be hearing things that are counter to your

00:53:06.539 --> 00:53:10.549
instincts, beliefs and whatever that you need to process.

00:53:10.569 --> 00:53:13.239
And eventually you will process them if you're hearing them.

00:53:13.239 --> 00:53:17.389
"I will guarantee you, you will ignore them if brought to

00:53:17.409 --> 00:53:20.420
you second and third hand." That's why hiring consultants to

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:23.809
go do this, etc. - consultants can make intros for you and

00:53:23.829 --> 00:53:26.389
help you organize the process, but you personally need to be

00:53:26.389 --> 00:53:27.389
hearing that data.

00:53:27.389 --> 00:53:33.379
By the way - Yes?

00:53:33.399 --> 00:53:34.399
[inaudible]

00:53:33.399 --> 00:53:34.399
Yep.

00:53:33.649 --> 00:53:34.649
[inaudible]

00:53:34.649 --> 00:53:45.150
Yeah. Go read the blog. There's a ton of it there - a ton.

00:53:45.170 --> 00:53:47.920
And there are lots of people that are writing about this

00:53:47.920 --> 00:53:50.670
now, but yes. And in fact, at the end I'll point you to some

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:51.670
resources.

00:53:51.670 --> 00:53:52.670
[inaudible].

00:53:52.670 --> 00:53:54.420
Yeah. There's a tab on the blog called "Startup Tools" and

00:53:54.420 --> 00:54:00.529
it links to the sites tools on how to build technically a

00:54:00.549 --> 00:54:04.440
startup. There's a ton of links to other blogs that talk

00:54:04.460 --> 00:54:06.509
about it.

00:54:06.529 --> 00:54:09.940
By the way, our old friend the business plan sometimes rears

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:12.480
its head here going, "Well, shouldn't I be just executing

00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:18.269
off my plan?" I'm just going to say this one more time. No

00:54:18.289 --> 00:54:20.469
business plan survives first contact with customers.

00:54:20.489 --> 00:54:26.509
Business plans come from large companies. They are great in

00:54:26.529 --> 00:54:30.279
large companies. They're great for professors who consult

00:54:30.299 --> 00:54:34.759
for large companies in business schools. Seriously. No

00:54:34.779 --> 00:54:37.750
professor I will contend to actually did a startup can look

00:54:37.769 --> 00:54:40.869
you in the eye and tell you this is a useful document. And

00:54:40.869 --> 00:54:43.799
because we never had anything to replace it with, you know,

00:54:43.819 --> 00:54:48.980
there was no other contest to run. But I'll show you we

00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:51.179
actually need to be looking for a business model.

00:54:51.179 --> 00:54:54.589
As I said earlier, if you put four professors in a room

00:54:54.609 --> 00:54:57.109
historically you'd come up with nine definitions of what a

00:54:57.109 --> 00:54:59.629
business model is cause everybody through that word around

00:54:59.649 --> 00:55:02.710
like it meant something. Now, for the first time I'm going

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:06.039
to show you what it means because of the work that I showed

00:55:06.059 --> 00:55:07.559
you in this other book in the beginning, Alexander

00:55:07.559 --> 00:55:08.559
Osterwalder.

00:55:08.559 --> 00:55:11.329
Osterwalder had a great insight. He said any company could

00:55:11.349 --> 00:55:14.480
be described in nine building blocks. And I'll give you the

00:55:14.500 --> 00:55:18.730
cliff notes of the book in a second. He said, "Listen, first

00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:22.819
building block is 'Who are your customers? What users are

00:55:22.839 --> 00:55:25.589
they serving? What jobs do they want to get done? What's

00:55:25.589 --> 00:55:27.819
your value proposition that you're giving them?" That's a

00:55:27.819 --> 00:55:33.049
fancy word for "What product or service are you building?"

00:55:33.069 --> 00:55:36.199
How are you getting it to them? Is it the web? Direct sales

00:55:36.199 --> 00:55:39.519
force? Indirect channel? And how are you creating demand?

00:55:39.539 --> 00:55:43.199
What kind of customer relationships will you have? And by

00:55:43.219 --> 00:55:46.449
the way, you add all this stuff up - what's your revenue

00:55:46.469 --> 00:55:52.109
model? Don't tell me free. If it's free it has to be a two

00:55:52.129 --> 00:55:54.900
sided market meaning, "Okay. It's free and finish the

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:57.710
sentence. And then we will make money by, you know,

00:55:57.730 --> 00:56:01.019
whatever. And it could be, "Well, we'll figure it out later.

00:56:01.039 --> 00:56:03.859
" "Alright. Well, that's just at least a declaration of

00:56:03.879 --> 00:56:06.319
faith. It's not actually a business model, but we could at

00:56:06.319 --> 00:56:08.449
least have that conversation."

00:56:08.469 --> 00:56:11.299
On the other side is, "What key resources do you need?" If

00:56:11.299 --> 00:56:14.449
it's a software company you need great engineers getting

00:56:14.469 --> 00:56:17.469
scarcer in Silicon Valley. If it's a hardware company do you

00:56:17.489 --> 00:56:21.480
need to go offshore? What do you need to do to perform it

00:56:21.500 --> 00:56:24.250
well? Is it manufacturing? Is it something else? Do you need

00:56:24.250 --> 00:56:29.369
any partners? iTunes - Without the record labels iPod would

00:56:29.389 --> 00:56:33.019
just have been another piece of hardware. Partners in some

00:56:33.039 --> 00:56:35.839
businesses are essential. And by the way, when you add up

00:56:35.859 --> 00:56:40.659
all the stuff on the left what's the cost structure? Nine

00:56:40.679 --> 00:56:43.400
building blocks can describe any startup now. Osterwalder's

00:56:43.420 --> 00:56:47.339
thing was a stroke of genius.

00:56:47.359 --> 00:56:51.079
And you can now take those and put it into a canvass that

00:56:51.099 --> 00:56:53.849
is, you know, we just took that fancy, nice drawing and

00:56:53.869 --> 00:56:57.109
said, "Hey! Let's just make this a canvass." And now, we'll

00:56:57.109 --> 00:57:01.129
take all our hypotheses. Remember those hypotheses I talked

00:57:01.149 --> 00:57:04.149
about? Your business is made up of a set of hypotheses? Let'

00:57:04.159 --> 00:57:06.789
s take the hypotheses about your product! Who's your

00:57:06.809 --> 00:57:11.759
product? What's the market type? What's your competition?

00:57:11.779 --> 00:57:14.710
Customers - what type of problem do you think they have?

00:57:14.730 --> 00:57:17.299
Who's the customer? Who's the user? Who's the payer? Who's

00:57:17.299 --> 00:57:22.339
the saboteur? Who's the influencer? What type of channel?

00:57:22.359 --> 00:57:26.079
And you add all these up and you can now use the Osterwalder

00:57:26.099 --> 00:57:30.250
business model diagram to actually become a score card for

00:57:30.269 --> 00:57:31.339
customer discovery.

00:57:31.359 --> 00:57:34.779
Remember I said, "Get out of the building?" Now you can

00:57:34.799 --> 00:57:37.549
actually say, "How are we doing on this?" Hey, wait a

00:57:37.549 --> 00:57:39.889
minute! Our first assumption of who the customer is? Oh,

00:57:39.889 --> 00:57:42.480
that was wrong! Customer is actually over here. Well, wait a

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:44.980
minute. Our pricing model we thought it was a premium

00:57:44.980 --> 00:57:47.480
business, now it should be a subscription business. Boom! We

00:57:47.480 --> 00:57:50.480
can change that. It's just a nice, visual way to show us

00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:57.179
over time what we're learning.

00:57:57.199 --> 00:58:03.460
The goal for engineering during this time is to build what's

00:58:03.480 --> 00:58:07.269
called the minimum viable product. Minimum viable product is

00:58:07.289 --> 00:58:11.719
the smallest feature set that gets you something and you get

00:58:11.739 --> 00:58:15.000
to define what that something is. Is it the minimum feature

00:58:15.019 --> 00:58:18.019
set that gets me orders or is it the minimum feature set

00:58:18.019 --> 00:58:21.269
that just gets me learning? I want to put up just a red

00:58:21.269 --> 00:58:24.769
button on my webpage to see if I can get anybody to push it.

00:58:24.769 --> 00:58:27.519
Truly, that could be a minimum viable product for week one

00:58:27.519 --> 00:58:30.519
in a startup. "Can I create demand to get people to this

00:58:30.519 --> 00:58:34.420
page?" or it could be, "No, I know what the core features of

00:58:34.440 --> 00:58:37.389
our website should be and are offering." Great! Do those. Or

00:58:37.409 --> 00:58:42.239
is it to get feedback? Or is it to test some failure modes?

00:58:42.259 --> 00:58:44.429
The first two things you need to be testing in that

00:58:44.429 --> 00:58:47.929
Osterwalder diagram is the minimal viable product and who

00:58:47.949 --> 00:58:51.429
that customer is. But remember, it's just one of those nine

00:58:51.449 --> 00:58:57.079
parts of that business model. And you're doing this customer

00:58:57.099 --> 00:59:00.599
discovery and validation, and again keeping score with the

00:59:00.619 --> 00:59:02.659
Osterwalder diagram.

00:59:02.679 --> 00:59:06.489
Now, there's an arrow on the bottom called the pivot. This

00:59:06.509 --> 00:59:09.969
is actually the heart of customer development and it's

00:59:09.989 --> 00:59:13.059
actually what we got wrong for the last 40 years of

00:59:13.079 --> 00:59:16.469
startups. Remember I kept saying, "Startups are not smaller

00:59:16.489 --> 00:59:20.059
versions of large companies"? Well, we never acted like that

00:59:20.079 --> 00:59:23.079
and here's an example why because any of you that have ever

00:59:23.079 --> 00:59:25.579
been in a startup are going to recognize this scenario.

00:59:25.579 --> 00:59:29.029
Startup goes through a launch first customership, has a

00:59:29.049 --> 00:59:32.469
great board meeting, everybody's high fiving each other

00:59:32.489 --> 00:59:35.549
because Tekron heard about your company, you know, people

00:59:35.569 --> 00:59:39.379
are hitting the website, things are wonderful.

00:59:39.399 --> 00:59:43.940
Next board meeting,

00:59:43.960 --> 00:59:47.649
[inaudible] VP of Sales. How's the number? - Referring to

00:59:47.669 --> 00:59:51.889
the revenue plan. VP of Sales says, "Oh! That sales pipeline

00:59:51.909 --> 00:59:56.119
is great." What does that mean? A website could be, "Well,

00:59:56.139 --> 00:59:58.699
we got lots of clicks, but it didn't quite convert

00:59:58.699 --> 01:00:01.419
[inaudible] could be, "You know, we got lots of people to

01:00:01.419 --> 01:00:04.429
talk to us, but we're not quite getting any orders."

01:00:04.449 --> 01:00:07.629
Next board meeting, "How we doing?" VP of Sales goes, "

01:00:07.649 --> 01:00:13.519
Pipeline." Next board meeting, "How we doing?" "Pipeline."

01:00:13.539 --> 01:00:19.460
And by the way, the next board meeting the VC's unbeknownst

01:00:19.480 --> 01:00:23.909
to you go to school to learn how to cock their heads at a 15

01:00:23.929 --> 01:00:28.190
degree angle and arch one eyebrow. I mean, it's just amazing

01:00:28.210 --> 01:00:33.259
because they do it, you know, in whatever that word is, but

01:00:33.279 --> 01:00:37.099
all together at the same time and they stare at the CEO. "

01:00:37.119 --> 01:00:39.869
Really? Revenue plan."

01:00:39.889 --> 01:00:43.849
The next board meeting you open the door and I don't know

01:00:43.869 --> 01:00:48.409
how they do it, but there's a flaming sword of death over

01:00:48.429 --> 01:00:51.969
the VP of Sales and no one is sitting next to her. I mean,

01:00:51.989 --> 01:00:55.859
all the chairs are on one side of the room and she knows

01:00:55.879 --> 01:00:59.129
that the wrong thing to say. And you shut the door very

01:00:59.149 --> 01:01:02.960
quickly because you open it again and she's gone. It's just

01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:05.739
amazing. It's like a magic trick. It happens all the time.

01:01:05.739 --> 01:01:09.289
And by the way, next board meeting you open the door again

01:01:09.309 --> 01:01:12.309
and there's someone else. Just looks like her, but it was a

01:01:12.309 --> 01:01:15.079
guy. You know, you're like, "What happened?" "We got a new

01:01:15.079 --> 01:01:16.079
VP of Sales."

01:01:16.079 --> 01:01:18.579
Now, the interesting part here - some of you might recognize

01:01:18.579 --> 01:01:23.039
this story - is that the new VP of Sales unless that guy and

01:01:23.059 --> 01:01:26.109
the CEO are complete idiots - does not execute the same

01:01:26.129 --> 01:01:28.899
sales strategy as the last one. They go, "Gee! That was

01:01:28.919 --> 01:01:31.669
stupid! And it was just because they were idiots! We're

01:01:31.679 --> 01:01:35.049
going to do another strategy!" What happens if that new

01:01:35.069 --> 01:01:41.239
strategy doesn't work? Who gets fired next? No, not the CEO.

01:01:41.259 --> 01:01:45.670
It must be a marketing problem. None of you are laughing,

01:01:45.690 --> 01:01:48.730
but yeah. It has to be a marketing problem. And then we fire

01:01:48.730 --> 01:01:49.730
the CEO.

01:01:49.730 --> 01:01:53.589
What did I just describe here? What I just described here is

01:01:53.609 --> 01:01:57.049
as some of you might recognize the normal path on how we

01:01:57.069 --> 01:02:01.909
solve problems in a startup. And no one in 40 years

01:02:01.929 --> 01:02:07.940
recognized, "Wait a minute! This occurs more often than the

01:02:07.960 --> 01:02:13.250
case that we actually execute the plan pristinely." In fact,

01:02:13.269 --> 01:02:19.519
in almost every startup it doesn't occur or it doesn't roll

01:02:19.539 --> 01:02:27.199
out per the plan. This is a huge idea. Startups are all

01:02:27.219 --> 01:02:33.129
about chaos and things not working like you believe. Your

01:02:33.149 --> 01:02:39.389
hypotheses are almost always wrong in a startup. That is

01:02:39.409 --> 01:02:43.960
they're not often right, yet the way we used to deal with

01:02:43.980 --> 01:02:50.210
this is by firing executives to iterate the strategy. Whoa!

01:02:50.230 --> 01:02:53.500
What a waste in time and money.

01:02:53.519 --> 01:02:58.690
This feedback loop - the pivot - simply says, "Being wrong

01:02:58.710 --> 01:03:04.149
is a normal part of the startup. Getting your hypotheses

01:03:04.169 --> 01:03:09.119
wrong is normal. So, what we're going to do is instead of

01:03:09.139 --> 01:03:12.690
firing people we're going to say, 'You know, we got

01:03:12.710 --> 01:03:18.179
something wrong in our discovery process. We just misheard

01:03:18.199 --> 01:03:20.440
what the market said because we thought they said if we

01:03:20.440 --> 01:03:24.909
delivered x they'd pay y. Don't work that way. Okay. We

01:03:24.929 --> 01:03:28.299
didn't hire 15 people on a sales force. We haven't hired,

01:03:28.319 --> 01:03:31.319
you know, 100 other people in support. Let's just go

01:03:31.339 --> 01:03:34.949
iterate." That's what the pivots for. Make sense?

01:03:34.969 --> 01:03:35.969
So wrap up -

01:03:35.969 --> 01:03:38.069
Startups are not small versions of large companies.

01:03:38.089 --> 01:03:41.460
Traditional big planning tools fail. And startups are built

01:03:41.480 --> 01:03:45.309
on hypotheses. You need to test each one of them. Business

01:03:45.329 --> 01:03:47.789
models help you keep score. Customer development is the

01:03:47.809 --> 01:03:50.089
process to test the hypotheses.

01:03:50.109 --> 01:03:53.359
Now, I've got about 11 more minutes. Can I tell you one more

01:03:53.359 --> 01:03:56.109
short story? You sure? Anybody wants to leave you can. But

01:03:56.119 --> 01:03:59.179
there's one more story of why startups aren't run by

01:03:59.199 --> 01:04:01.759
accountants. Anybody an accountant here? I don't want to

01:04:01.779 --> 01:04:03.589
complain. You can leave now.

01:04:03.609 --> 01:04:07.960
Anybody know who the inventor of the modern corporation is?

01:04:07.980 --> 01:04:11.029
Look at this guy. Doesn't he look serious? This is actually

01:04:11.049 --> 01:04:15.039
a real Time magazine cover from the 1920's. Anybody know who

01:04:15.059 --> 01:04:19.739
he is? Who was he? Yeah. I'm glad you can read. Who was he?

01:04:19.759 --> 01:04:22.289
What about General Motors? Who was he?

01:04:22.309 --> 01:04:23.309
[inaudible]

01:04:22.309 --> 01:04:23.309
Good.

01:04:23.309 --> 01:04:36.289
[inaudible]. Anybody else? Yeah. That's what most people

01:04:36.309 --> 01:04:38.559
think of him. Alfred P. Sloan, General Motors President &amp;

01:04:38.559 --> 01:04:42.149
Chairman - he actually invented the cost accounting system

01:04:42.169 --> 01:04:45.629
for running large corporations. Corporations in the 20th

01:04:45.649 --> 01:04:50.449
century are basically possible because this guy took the

01:04:50.469 --> 01:04:53.899
accounting system that DuPont used in World War I - who was

01:04:53.919 --> 01:04:57.969
on his board as you'll hear later - and actually applied it

01:04:57.989 --> 01:05:02.239
into making GM a large distributor organization. Put the

01:05:02.259 --> 01:05:05.730
managerial and financial controls - and it was copied by

01:05:05.750 --> 01:05:08.029
every other major corporation in the United States pre -

01:05:08.049 --> 01:05:11.359
World War II and post - World War II led to the American

01:05:11.379 --> 01:05:14.559
Century in terms of corporate expansion overseas. Truly a

01:05:14.579 --> 01:05:20.039
genius. Lots of things are named after him - MIT Sloan

01:05:20.059 --> 01:05:22.239
School, Sloan Foundation, Sloan-Kettering Cancer Institute

01:05:22.259 --> 01:05:23.259
in New York.

01:05:23.259 --> 01:05:27.169
Everybody know who Kettering was? Oh. Another fact, he was

01:05:27.189 --> 01:05:32.839
his VP of Engineering, of course, so great guy. And in some

01:05:32.859 --> 01:05:35.859
classes I have people say, "Why yes! He was the founder of

01:05:35.859 --> 01:05:40.079
GM." Turns out Sloan had nothing to do with the founding of

01:05:40.099 --> 01:05:44.000
GM by 20 years. This guy was the founder. Doesn't he look

01:05:44.019 --> 01:05:46.619
like a founder? Come on! Which guy would you rather hang out

01:05:46.619 --> 01:05:50.739
with? This guy, you know, or this guy. This guy we can take

01:05:50.759 --> 01:05:53.759
in the streets of Palo Alto one night. "Hey! Come on! We're

01:05:53.759 --> 01:05:54.759
going on a barge!"

01:05:54.759 --> 01:05:59.730
This was Billy Durant. Billy Durant was the founder of

01:05:59.750 --> 01:06:04.309
General Motors. Billy Durant also was the leader in the

01:06:04.329 --> 01:06:08.319
United States in horse drawn buggies in the 1890's out of

01:06:08.339 --> 01:06:11.659
Flint, Michigan. Built the largest horse drawn buggy company

01:06:11.679 --> 01:06:17.569
in the U.S. One day Billy Durant was in the bar in Flint

01:06:17.589 --> 01:06:20.839
with his fellow buggy manufacturers. Probably Friday

01:06:20.859 --> 01:06:24.679
afternoon, knocking a couple back and all of a sudden they

01:06:24.699 --> 01:06:27.960
hear this noise coming up this dirt covered street. It's

01:06:27.980 --> 01:06:30.859
like the loudest thing they've ever heard. And all of a

01:06:30.859 --> 01:06:33.609
sudden they see this carriage coming at them. It wasn't even

01:06:33.609 --> 01:06:36.609
one of Durant's. It was one of his competitors and it didn't

01:06:36.609 --> 01:06:39.539
have any horses. And somebody had strapped a one cylinder

01:06:39.559 --> 01:06:44.409
engine to it - 1890's - and basically, you know, called it

01:06:44.429 --> 01:06:45.429
transportation.

01:06:45.429 --> 01:06:48.389
And the rest of his friends were hysterical because this

01:06:48.409 --> 01:06:50.909
thing looked like it was going to catch fire anytime. Horses

01:06:50.909 --> 01:06:54.309
were running wild, you know? And they were all, "Hey Billy!

01:06:54.329 --> 01:06:59.279
Look at that!" Durant sobered up in about 30 seconds. Next

01:06:59.299 --> 01:07:02.399
Monday he sold his entire business - got out of the buggy

01:07:02.419 --> 01:07:07.029
business - and started buying technology companies of the

01:07:07.049 --> 01:07:10.259
time - highest tech companies of the 1890's, automobile

01:07:10.279 --> 01:07:13.449
companies. He was not a technologist, but this guy was an

01:07:13.469 --> 01:07:14.730
entrepreneur.

01:07:14.750 --> 01:07:19.179
And Billy Durant bought up a series of car companies and

01:07:19.199 --> 01:07:23.319
starts a company called General Motors, builds it into the

01:07:23.339 --> 01:07:27.929
largest automobile company in the United States in 1910 and

01:07:27.949 --> 01:07:31.039
then is fired by his board because he's a crazy

01:07:31.059 --> 01:07:36.879
entrepreneur, insane, careless, reckless, etc. But he's also

01:07:36.899 --> 01:07:40.869
pissed. He's so angry at his board for firing him he says, "

01:07:40.889 --> 01:07:44.869
Screw you guys", finds another entrepreneur and starts

01:07:44.889 --> 01:07:48.829
another car company called Chevrolet. Builds Chevrolet into

01:07:48.849 --> 01:07:51.489
a bigger brand and company than General Motors, takes it

01:07:51.509 --> 01:07:55.359
public, and buys out all the GM stock, fires his board and

01:07:55.379 --> 01:07:58.369
takes over his company again.

01:07:58.389 --> 01:08:02.599
Remember a guy named Steve Jobs? Runs his company for

01:08:02.619 --> 01:08:12.069
another 10 years in 1921. GM is $3.6 billion in today's

01:08:12.089 --> 01:08:15.589
daughters being run by his company's founder and he screws

01:08:15.609 --> 01:08:17.710
up again financially. This time he's gambling with the

01:08:17.710 --> 01:08:21.709
company stock. I mean, clearly crazy entrepreneur. And this

01:08:21.729 --> 01:08:24.729
time the financiers he has on the board aren't some local

01:08:24.750 --> 01:08:29.169
yokels. It is the DuPont's and they fire him for the second

01:08:29.189 --> 01:08:30.189
time.

01:08:30.189 --> 01:08:34.259
Durant gets fired from General Motors in 1921. The company

01:08:34.279 --> 01:08:43.719
is $3.6 billion - Durant vs. Sloan. Sloan becomes the icon

01:08:43.739 --> 01:08:46.489
of American management - rightly so because from the 1920's

01:08:46.510 --> 01:08:51.169
- 1950's the American Century truly is the GM century. He

01:08:51.189 --> 01:08:53.109
dies rich, honored and famous.

01:08:53.130 --> 01:09:00.019
Durant? He dies penniless managing a bowling alley in 1947.

01:09:00.039 --> 01:09:02.439
He was the accountant. You are here. Thank you very much.

01:09:02.439 --> 01:09:16.799
So, I've got time if anybody has any questions about God

01:09:16.819 --> 01:09:46.819
knows what I said, but any questions? Yes?

01:11:05.809 --> 01:11:06.809
[Informal talk]

